//   graphs/algebraic-geometry/data/comprehensibility.js
//   graphs/arithmetic-geometry/data/comprehensibility.js
// Comprehensibility of statements and definitions (window.AG_COMPR).
//
// The rating is about the prior knowledge a reader needs to understand what a theorem asserts or what a definition
// fixes. How hard the proof is does not matter. Five levels:
//   1 very high School mathematics (integers, primes, equations, geometry in the plane and in space).
//   2 high Upper secondary school and first semesters (complex numbers, arithmetic modulo p, limits, polynomials in
//   several
//                  variables, linear algebra, projective plane).
//   3 medium Bachelor's level (groups, rings, field extensions, holomorphic functions, Riemann surfaces,
//                  projective varieties).
//   4 low Master's level (sheaves, cohomology, schemes, divisors and bundles, abelian varieties).
//   5 very low Research level (derived categories, stacks, étale and ℓ-adic cohomology, deformation theory,
//                  minimal model program for pairs).
//
// Rule for the most accessible version: if a statement or definition has a version proved to be equivalent (relation
// „aequivalent“ in daten.js or a well-known reformulation, e.g. the elliptic curve as a Weierstrass equation instead
// of a curve of genus 1 with a point), the most accessible of these versions counts. It is given in the field
// `version`.
// Special cases do not count as equivalent versions; at most they are mentioned in `reason`.
//
//   theorems id (from daten.js and daten-gross.js, all types: foundation, theorem, conjecture) -> { level, version?,
//   reason }
//   concepts id (from begriffe.js) -> { level, version?, reason }
//   `reason` states the required prior knowledge in one short sentence.
//
// The rating is an editorial judgment, not a measurement, and has not been checked by an expert. For comparison,
// verstaendlichkeit-filter.js computes the definitional depth of the statement from begriffe.js.

