// Comprehensibility of the statements and definitions (window.AR_COMPR).
//
// The rating reflects the prior knowledge a reader needs to understand what a theorem claims or what a definition
// fixes. How hard the proof is plays no role. Five levels:
//   1 very high School mathematics (integers, prime numbers, divisibility, equations, logarithm).
//   2 high Upper secondary school and first semesters (complex numbers, arithmetic modulo p, limits and O-notation,
//   series,
//                  integrals, polynomials).
//   3 medium Bachelor's studies (groups, rings, field extensions, holomorphic functions and analytic
//                  continuation, Riemann surfaces, number fields).
//   4 low Master's studies (modular forms with Hecke theory, Galois groups and Galois representations, schemes,
//                  cohomology).
//   5 very low Research level (automorphic representations, deformations of Galois representations, Selmer groups,
//                  ℓ-adic cohomology, Brauer–Manin obstruction).
//
// Rule for the most accessible version: if a statement or definition has a provably equivalent version (relation
// „aequivalent“ in daten.js or a known reformulation, such as the elliptic curve as a Weierstrass equation instead of
// as a curve of genus 1 with a point), the most accessible of these versions counts. It is stored in the field
// `version`.
// Special cases and weaker statements do not count as equivalent versions.
//
//   theorems id (from daten.js, all types: foundation, theorem, conjecture) -> { level, version?, reason }
//   concepts id (from begriffe.js) -> { level, version?, reason }
//   `reason` names the required prior knowledge in one short sentence.
//
// The rating is an editorial assessment, not a measurement, and has not been checked by an expert. For comparison,
// ../Beispiel Algebraische Geometrie/verstaendlichkeit-filter.js computes the definitional depth of the statement from
// begriffe.js.

